1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,514
A pull request fixes a typo in
the README. It has an approval.

2
00:00:08,554 --> 00:00:14,007
It has been open for three days, because
one required check, a test result the rules

3
00:00:14,047 --> 00:00:19,238
demand under the name build, says "Expected,
waiting for status to be reported".

4
00:00:19,278 --> 00:00:22,769
Nobody can find a failed run.
Nobody can find any run.

5
00:00:23,109 --> 00:00:25,298
Your CTO asks: what is broken?

6
00:00:25,638 --> 00:00:30,465
Nothing in Git, and nothing in the workflow,
the file that tells GitHub which jobs

7
00:00:30,505 --> 00:00:35,391
to run. The workflow has a path filter, so
for a documentation-only change it never

8
00:00:35,431 --> 00:00:38,418
starts. A workflow that
never starts never reports.

9
00:00:38,458 --> 00:00:43,343
And the rule on main does not ask "did CI
fail?" It asks "is there a passing result

10
00:00:43,383 --> 00:00:48,210
under the name build on the newest commit?"
There is not, and there never will be.

11
00:00:48,550 --> 00:00:52,319
That is the most common of about
ten documented traps of one rule.

12
00:00:52,359 --> 00:00:56,936
This video goes through the rules that
cause nearly all blocked merges, with the

13
00:00:56,976 --> 00:01:01,669
local command that predicts each verdict.
And the README pull request gets its fix

14
00:01:01,709 --> 00:01:02,650
before we finish.

15
00:01:04,490 --> 00:01:08,337
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

16
00:01:08,377 --> 00:01:13,498
In the last video a rule was a condition
on a ref update, a ruleset was a list of

17
00:01:13,538 --> 00:01:17,895
rules with a target, a status and a
bypass list, and layers added up.

18
00:01:17,935 --> 00:01:19,233
Now we open the list.

19
00:01:19,573 --> 00:01:23,897
A branch or tag ruleset can contain
about a dozen kinds of rule.

20
00:01:23,937 --> 00:01:29,558
Four of them, pull request, status checks,
signed commits and linear history, cause

21
00:01:29,598 --> 00:01:35,218
nearly all blocked merges. Sections 18.6
to 18.11 take those, and two smaller ones:

22
00:01:35,258 --> 00:01:39,037
conversation resolution, and
force pushes and deletions.

23
00:01:39,377 --> 00:01:44,323
The demonstration is a local pre-flight:
labs/ch18/merge-preflight.sh.

24
00:01:44,363 --> 00:01:50,165
It asks Git the questions that can be
decided from Git data alone, before you ever

25
00:01:50,205 --> 00:01:56,006
open the merge box. Every command in it
reads state and is 🟢 SAFE, except its last

26
00:01:56,046 --> 00:01:58,570
step, git merge, which is 🟡 CAUTION.

27
00:01:58,910 --> 00:02:04,117
Then a walkthrough of Lab 23.3 on the
platform. That lab creates and edits rulesets

28
00:02:04,157 --> 00:02:09,236
with gh api calls that are not GET. The
chapter labels those 🔴 DANGEROUS, for the

29
00:02:09,276 --> 00:02:14,293
reason given in the last video: the call
does whatever the endpoint and the JSON

30
00:02:14,333 --> 00:02:16,252
say, with all your permissions.

31
00:02:18,092 --> 00:02:19,504
After this video you can:

32
00:02:19,844 --> 00:02:23,834
List the rules a ruleset offers and
the sub-options of required reviews.

33
00:02:24,174 --> 00:02:28,371
Explain why a required check can stay
pending forever and design the fix.

34
00:02:28,711 --> 00:02:31,537
Say what "require branches
to be up to date" tests.

35
00:02:31,877 --> 00:02:35,968
Explain why a merge method can be blocked
by "Require signed commits" and which

36
00:02:36,008 --> 00:02:38,216
merge methods a
linear-history rule allows.

37
00:02:38,556 --> 00:02:41,783
Run the local pre-flight that
predicts each rule's verdict.

38
00:02:43,623 --> 00:02:49,237
The catalogue, quickly. Restrict creations,
restrict updates, restrict deletions:

39
00:02:49,277 --> 00:02:53,703
only bypass actors may create,
push to, or delete matching refs.

40
00:02:53,743 --> 00:02:59,426
Block force pushes. Require linear history.
Require a pull request before merging.

41
00:02:59,466 --> 00:03:03,195
Require status checks to
pass. Require signed commits.

42
00:03:03,235 --> 00:03:08,430
Require deployments to succeed. Require
merge queue, documented only in the

43
00:03:08,470 --> 00:03:09,965
Enterprise Cloud view.

44
00:03:10,305 --> 00:03:15,252
Rules for code scanning and related
results. Automatically request Copilot code

45
00:03:15,292 --> 00:03:19,796
review, which requests and does not
block. Require workflows to pass, at

46
00:03:19,836 --> 00:03:25,098
organization or enterprise level. And metadata
restrictions, on the Enterprise plan:

47
00:03:25,138 --> 00:03:30,337
patterns for commit messages, author and
committer email, and branch and tag names.

48
00:03:33,677 --> 00:03:38,668
A new ruleset starts with two rules already
selected: restrict deletions and block

49
00:03:38,708 --> 00:03:41,367
force pushes. Everything
else is a decision.

50
00:03:41,707 --> 00:03:46,670
Required reviews. In one sentence: the pull
request rule turns the advisory reviews

51
00:03:46,710 --> 00:03:51,491
of the pull request chapter into a gate,
and each sub-option closes one specific

52
00:03:51,531 --> 00:03:55,891
loophole. Note first what the rule
itself says: "The pull request doesn't

53
00:03:55,931 --> 00:03:59,266
necessarily have to be approved,
but it must be opened."

54
00:03:59,606 --> 00:04:04,306
The sub-options, each with the loophole
it closes. Required approvals: merging

55
00:04:04,346 --> 00:04:09,167
without a second person. Approvals count
only from people with write permission.

56
00:04:09,207 --> 00:04:12,752
Dismiss stale approvals: commits
pushed after the approval.

57
00:04:12,792 --> 00:04:17,613
Approval of the most recent reviewable
push: the last pusher approving their own

58
00:04:17,653 --> 00:04:19,921
addition. It is weaker than dismissal.

59
00:04:20,261 --> 00:04:25,349
Review from code owners: changes to owned
paths without their owner, and any one

60
00:04:25,389 --> 00:04:30,606
listed owner suffices. Restrict who can
dismiss reviews: someone with write access

61
00:04:30,646 --> 00:04:35,669
dismissing a blocking review. It is in
rulesets since the seventh of July 2026.

62
00:04:35,709 --> 00:04:39,002
Resolved conversations:
merging over open questions.

63
00:04:39,342 --> 00:04:43,571
Allowed merge methods: history
written in a form the team rejected.

64
00:04:43,611 --> 00:04:47,775
Required reviewers: paths that need
a specific team, with a count.

65
00:04:47,815 --> 00:04:52,426
Teams only, organization repositories
only, generally available since the

66
00:04:52,466 --> 00:04:54,273
seventeenth of February 2026.

67
00:04:54,613 --> 00:04:58,778
Three rules of evaluation that the
documentation states and people miss.

68
00:04:58,818 --> 00:05:03,568
Under this rule, a request for changes now
blocks: that person must approve before

69
00:05:03,608 --> 00:05:08,357
the merge, or someone with write permission
dismisses the review, unless dismissal

70
00:05:08,397 --> 00:05:13,263
is restricted. Twin pull requests block each
other: you cannot merge if another open

71
00:05:13,303 --> 00:05:18,111
pull request has a head branch pointing to
the same commit with pending or rejected

72
00:05:18,151 --> 00:05:18,578
reviews.

73
00:05:18,918 --> 00:05:24,360
And method conflicts block: a ruleset that
allows only squash, in a repository where

74
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:27,950
squash merging is switched
off, leaves no way to merge.

75
00:05:28,290 --> 00:05:33,362
One caveat marked unverified: the
documentation describes the file patterns of

76
00:05:33,402 --> 00:05:38,213
required reviewers as .gitignore-like,
while the REST schema says fnmatch.

77
00:05:38,253 --> 00:05:42,015
The two do not agree. Test a
pattern before relying on it.

78
00:05:42,355 --> 00:05:47,217
Required status checks. In one sentence:
the rule holds a list of check names.

79
00:05:47,257 --> 00:05:52,246
A merge is allowed when the newest commit
has a passing result under every name,

80
00:05:52,286 --> 00:05:53,817
from whoever reported it.

81
00:05:54,157 --> 00:05:58,043
Read that sentence again,
because every trap is inside it.

82
00:05:58,083 --> 00:06:01,157
Names. The newest commit.
Whoever reported it.

83
00:06:01,497 --> 00:06:07,464
Quick quiz, with the hook in mind. A path
filter keeps the workflow from starting.

84
00:06:07,504 --> 00:06:11,712
What does the required check
show? A: success. B: failure.

85
00:06:11,752 --> 00:06:14,202
C: pending, for ever. Your answer?

86
00:06:19,042 --> 00:06:23,500
C. Nothing ran, so nothing reported,
and the rule keeps waiting for a name.

87
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:28,680
Strict or loose. "Strict" means "Require
branches to be up to date before merging"

88
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:32,846
is selected: the topic branch must
be up to date with the base branch.

89
00:06:32,886 --> 00:06:36,536
Loose means fewer builds, and
checks may fail after you merge.

90
00:06:36,576 --> 00:06:41,536
"Up to date" is a question about ancestry:
is the tip of the base an ancestor of the

91
00:06:41,576 --> 00:06:46,535
head? You can ask Git that. In the picture
the answer is no: main has one commit the

92
00:06:46,575 --> 00:06:47,309
branch lacks.

93
00:06:47,649 --> 00:06:52,716
The traps, all documented. A skipped workflow:
"If a workflow is skipped due to path

94
00:06:52,756 --> 00:06:57,641
filtering, branch filtering or a commit
message, then checks associated with that

95
00:06:57,681 --> 00:07:02,262
workflow will remain in a 'Pending'
state." The pull request waits for ever.

96
00:07:02,302 --> 00:07:06,822
A skipped job: "A job that is skipped
will report its status as 'Success'."

97
00:07:07,162 --> 00:07:12,398
A required check can be green without having
run. [skip ci] in the head commit: the

98
00:07:12,438 --> 00:07:15,957
workflow does not run, and
required checks stay pending.

99
00:07:15,997 --> 00:07:20,915
The seven-day rule: a check name cannot
be picked in the settings until it has

100
00:07:20,955 --> 00:07:25,237
completed successfully in the
repository during the past seven days.

101
00:07:25,577 --> 00:07:29,850
The newest commit: every push and
every "Update branch" starts over.

102
00:07:29,890 --> 00:07:34,988
The same name twice: if a check and a
commit status have the same name, both must

103
00:07:35,028 --> 00:07:40,253
pass. The wrong event: checks from workflow
jobs count only for runs triggered by a

104
00:07:40,293 --> 00:07:44,884
listed set of events. A green manual
run on the branch satisfies nothing.

105
00:07:48,224 --> 00:07:51,233
The merge queue: its groups
trigger a separate event.

106
00:07:51,273 --> 00:07:55,029
The wrong source: a check can be
pinned to an expected GitHub App.

107
00:07:55,069 --> 00:07:59,402
And the row that makes pinning matter:
"Any person or integration with write

108
00:07:59,442 --> 00:08:03,256
permissions to a repository can set
the state of any status check."

109
00:08:03,296 --> 00:08:06,650
An unpinned required check can
be satisfied by an API call.

110
00:08:06,990 --> 00:08:12,597
Check names: for a workflow job the name is
the job name, and required status checks

111
00:08:12,637 --> 00:08:16,967
do not take workflow, matrix or
event trigger types into account.

112
00:08:17,007 --> 00:08:22,345
So make job names unique across all
workflows. One caveat marked unverified: the

113
00:08:22,385 --> 00:08:26,850
exact check name a matrix job reports
is not stated on these pages.

114
00:08:26,890 --> 00:08:32,497
Do not guess it. Read the names from a real
run with gh pr checks, or from the merge

115
00:08:32,537 --> 00:08:34,581
box, and require what you read.

116
00:08:34,921 --> 00:08:39,876
The fix for the hook. The textbook calls
it the robust pattern and is explicit that

117
00:08:39,916 --> 00:08:44,028
it is an inference from the documented
rows, not a documented recipe.

118
00:08:44,068 --> 00:08:49,022
Let the workflow start on every pull request.
Decide inside the workflow which jobs

119
00:08:49,062 --> 00:08:54,077
do real work. And require one final job that
depends on the others, runs always, and

120
00:08:54,117 --> 00:08:58,831
fails if any of them failed or was
cancelled. One stable name, never skipped at

121
00:08:58,871 --> 00:09:03,043
workflow level. The path optimization
survives, as conditions on jobs.

122
00:09:03,383 --> 00:09:08,022
Conversation resolution. Every review
thread must be marked resolved before the

123
00:09:08,062 --> 00:09:12,938
merge. The control is weaker than it sounds:
resolving a thread is a click, and the

124
00:09:12,978 --> 00:09:17,794
author may do it. The rule ensures that
every thread was acknowledged, not that it

125
00:09:17,834 --> 00:09:18,624
was addressed.

126
00:09:18,964 --> 00:09:23,508
Signed commits. In one sentence: the rule
accepts only commits with a verified

127
00:09:23,548 --> 00:09:28,152
signature on the target, and it is evaluated
against the commits a pull request

128
00:09:28,192 --> 00:09:31,561
introduces, not only against
the commit the merge creates.

129
00:09:31,901 --> 00:09:36,744
The part that surprises teams, in GitHub's
words: "unsigned commits on the head

130
00:09:36,784 --> 00:09:41,626
branch can block a squash merge, even
though GitHub would sign the final squash

131
00:09:41,666 --> 00:09:46,446
commit". So, method by method. Merge
commit: works if every commit on the head

132
00:09:46,486 --> 00:09:51,514
branch is signed and verified. Squash: the
result would pass, but unsigned commits

133
00:09:51,554 --> 00:09:56,149
on the head branch block the merge
anyway. Rebase: cannot satisfy the rule.

134
00:09:56,189 --> 00:10:00,599
The documented workaround is to rebase
and merge locally, and then push.

135
00:10:00,939 --> 00:10:05,852
When not to use it: the rule proves that
someone holding a registered key made each

136
00:10:05,892 --> 00:10:10,626
commit. It does not prove the commit is
good, and it costs every contributor and

137
00:10:10,666 --> 00:10:11,581
every bot a key.

138
00:10:11,921 --> 00:10:16,715
Linear history. The rule constrains what
lands on the target, not the shape of the

139
00:10:16,755 --> 00:10:21,667
pull request branch. A branch that contains
"Merge main into" commits, like this one

140
00:10:21,707 --> 00:10:26,325
with its new commit of two parents, is
fine if it is squash-merged, because one

141
00:10:26,365 --> 00:10:30,923
ordinary commit lands. Under this rule
the merge-commit method is not allowed.

142
00:10:31,263 --> 00:10:35,887
Block force pushes, with GitHub's security
argument: force pushing can "point a

143
00:10:35,927 --> 00:10:39,429
branch to commits that were not
approved in a pull request".

144
00:10:39,469 --> 00:10:43,679
Without this rule, a required pull
request can be undone after the fact.

145
00:10:43,719 --> 00:10:48,284
And restrict deletions keeps main and
release branches from being deleted by a

146
00:10:48,324 --> 00:10:49,111
mistyped push.

147
00:10:50,951 --> 00:10:55,466
Think of the merge box as a form with
one line per rule, and of each line as a

148
00:10:55,506 --> 00:11:00,255
question with a yes or no answer. The
mistake people make is to read the form as a

149
00:11:00,295 --> 00:11:03,642
description of their work: "CI
passed", "it was reviewed".

150
00:11:03,982 --> 00:11:07,109
The form does not describe
work. Each line is a lookup.

151
00:11:07,149 --> 00:11:10,564
Is there a passing result under
this name on this commit ID?

152
00:11:10,604 --> 00:11:14,250
Is there an approval that is still
attached to the current diff?

153
00:11:14,290 --> 00:11:17,647
Does every commit in this range
carry a verified signature?

154
00:11:17,987 --> 00:11:22,466
Half of those lookups you can do yourself,
because their inputs are Git data:

155
00:11:22,506 --> 00:11:27,220
ancestry, parents, signatures, paths. The
other half need the platform's records.

156
00:11:27,260 --> 00:11:31,446
Sorting each rule into one of the two
halves is the skill of this video.

157
00:11:31,786 --> 00:11:37,631
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. Two
columns: Git data, and platform records.

158
00:11:37,671 --> 00:11:43,157
Sort these four: up to date, required
approvals, linear history, conversation

159
00:11:43,197 --> 00:11:44,736
resolution. I'll wait.

160
00:11:49,576 --> 00:11:54,524
Up to date and linear history are Git data.
Approvals and conversations live in the

161
00:11:54,564 --> 00:11:55,666
platform's records.

162
00:11:56,006 --> 00:12:00,905
The model breaks for one rule: status
checks. There the lookup has a third outcome

163
00:12:00,945 --> 00:12:05,301
besides yes and no: no answer yet. And
"no answer yet" can last for ever.

164
00:12:07,141 --> 00:12:11,143
The top four rows are decided from
Git data, and each has a command.

165
00:12:11,183 --> 00:12:15,601
Signatures need one qualification: your
local verdict uses your local trust

166
00:12:15,641 --> 00:12:20,357
settings. "Verified" on GitHub needs the
public key registered with the account.

167
00:12:20,697 --> 00:12:24,831
The row for paths is a hybrid: Git gives
you the list of changed paths, and the

168
00:12:24,871 --> 00:12:29,112
platform decides what that list triggers:
which owners, which reviewers, and, for

169
00:12:29,152 --> 00:12:30,803
the hook, which workflows start.

170
00:12:31,143 --> 00:12:35,672
The bottom three rows need the platform.
No local command predicts an approval.

171
00:12:37,512 --> 00:12:41,725
You are on feature/priority-routing,
about to ask for a merge into main.

172
00:12:42,065 --> 00:12:47,317
Fresh information first. Every answer below
is about origin/main as of this fetch.

173
00:12:49,657 --> 00:12:54,097
The rule is "require branches to be up to
date". main gained one commit since you

174
00:12:54,137 --> 00:12:57,139
branched. What is the exit
status of the first command?

175
00:12:57,479 --> 00:13:01,454
Exit status 1: the tip of main is
not an ancestor of the branch.

176
00:13:01,494 --> 00:13:05,656
The count says one commit only main
has, three only the branch has.

177
00:13:05,996 --> 00:13:10,327
Here are those counts as a picture, taken
from the commit where the two histories

178
00:13:10,367 --> 00:13:14,805
diverged. Under strict checks, this pull
request cannot merge yet, however green it

179
00:13:14,845 --> 00:13:14,967
is.

180
00:13:15,307 --> 00:13:19,089
Mergeability: the test merge
succeeds, exit status 0.

181
00:13:19,129 --> 00:13:25,073
No conflict. Next, linear history. Predict
how many merge commits this pull request

182
00:13:25,113 --> 00:13:27,092
introduces. Say it out loud.

183
00:13:31,932 --> 00:13:36,983
The commits a rule inspects are those the
pull request introduces: three, each with

184
00:13:37,023 --> 00:13:41,829
one parent. Merge commits among them:
zero. Linear history is satisfied for any

185
00:13:41,869 --> 00:13:43,792
method that lands these commits.

186
00:13:46,132 --> 00:13:51,207
The rule is "require signed commits", and
the team merges by squash, which GitHub

187
00:13:51,247 --> 00:13:55,502
signs. These three commits are
unsigned. Can the pull request merge?

188
00:13:55,842 --> 00:14:00,819
N three times: no signature. And no: unsigned
commits on the head branch block the

189
00:14:00,859 --> 00:14:04,612
squash merge, even though GitHub
would sign the squash commit.

190
00:14:04,652 --> 00:14:09,384
The documented repair for unsigned commits
already pushed is to rebase them to

191
00:14:09,424 --> 00:14:12,198
include a verified
signature, then force push.

192
00:14:12,538 --> 00:14:16,655
Author and committer addresses, for
metadata rules on the Enterprise plan.

193
00:14:16,995 --> 00:14:21,806
The changed paths, with three dots. Code
owners, required reviewers by path, push

194
00:14:21,846 --> 00:14:26,777
rules and path filters all key on this list.
For the pull request in the hook, this

195
00:14:26,817 --> 00:14:31,569
command would print one line, README.md,
and you could read the workflow's paths

196
00:14:31,609 --> 00:14:35,162
filter against it and know that
the workflow will not start.

197
00:14:35,502 --> 00:14:37,815
Now fix "not up to date", variant 1.

198
00:14:40,155 --> 00:14:44,688
After merging main in: is the branch up
to date, and what does the merge-commit

199
00:14:44,728 --> 00:14:48,335
count say now? Under a linear-history
rule, can it still merge?

200
00:14:48,675 --> 00:14:53,128
Up to date: exit status 0, and
zero commits only main has.

201
00:14:53,468 --> 00:14:58,302
The branch now carries one merge commit.
Under the linear rule this pull request can

202
00:14:58,342 --> 00:15:01,957
still be squash-merged, because
the rule constrains what lands.

203
00:15:01,997 --> 00:15:05,148
Under the merge-commit method
the rule would refuse it.

204
00:15:05,488 --> 00:15:09,843
A third ruleset adds a required
status check named ci/unit, strict.

205
00:15:09,883 --> 00:15:15,288
Nothing in the repository reports that
check. It is created from a JSON file of the

206
00:15:15,328 --> 00:15:19,552
course. Read the file first, and
remember the label on this call.

207
00:15:19,892 --> 00:15:24,951
Then the lab opens pull requests that cannot
merge, each for a different reason, and

208
00:15:24,991 --> 00:15:28,410
you diagnose each by working
from the rules to the cause.

209
00:15:28,450 --> 00:15:33,266
For the first case, a required check that
nobody reports, look at the merge box:

210
00:15:33,306 --> 00:15:38,061
according to the documentation, the check
is listed as expected and waiting for

211
00:15:38,101 --> 00:15:41,764
status to be reported. Then use
the commands that read state:

212
00:15:42,104 --> 00:15:45,592
gh pr checks lists what has
reported on the head commit.

213
00:15:45,632 --> 00:15:49,436
gh ruleset check lists what the
rules require for the branch.

214
00:15:49,476 --> 00:15:54,603
The diagnosis is the difference between
the two lists: a name that is required and

215
00:15:54,643 --> 00:15:59,518
that nothing has reported. The lab also
demonstrates the last row of the traps

216
00:15:59,558 --> 00:16:04,370
table, that anyone with write permission
can set the state of a status check.

217
00:16:04,410 --> 00:16:08,592
Do that step only in your own
practice repository, as the lab says.

218
00:16:10,432 --> 00:16:12,080
Five mistakes to watch for.

219
00:16:12,420 --> 00:16:15,661
Requiring a check from a
workflow with a path filter.

220
00:16:15,701 --> 00:16:20,675
Root cause: a workflow skipped by a path
filter never reports, so its checks stay

221
00:16:20,715 --> 00:16:23,275
pending and the requirement cannot be met.

222
00:16:23,615 --> 00:16:27,350
Trusting a green required check
that came from a skipped job.

223
00:16:27,390 --> 00:16:29,950
Root cause: a skipped job reports success.

224
00:16:30,290 --> 00:16:35,015
Expecting a squash merge to pass "Require
signed commits" because GitHub signs the

225
00:16:35,055 --> 00:16:39,489
squash commit. Root cause: the rule is
evaluated against the commits the pull

226
00:16:39,529 --> 00:16:43,324
request introduces, including
unsigned commits on the head branch.

227
00:16:43,664 --> 00:16:48,127
Updating the branch and being surprised
that checks and approvals start over.

228
00:16:48,167 --> 00:16:52,864
Root cause: required checks must pass on
the latest commit, and the diff changed.

229
00:16:53,204 --> 00:16:56,553
Reading "resolved conversations"
as "addressed feedback".

230
00:16:56,593 --> 00:17:00,835
Root cause: the pull request author
can resolve threads; the rule proves

231
00:17:00,875 --> 00:17:02,084
acknowledgement only.

232
00:17:03,924 --> 00:17:07,829
Now, out of the lab. The README
pull request from the hook.

233
00:17:07,869 --> 00:17:13,445
The team's CI workflow starts only for
changes under src/ and tests/, to save runner

234
00:17:13,485 --> 00:17:18,995
minutes on documentation changes. The ruleset
on main requires the check build from

235
00:17:19,035 --> 00:17:24,343
that workflow. For three days the two
settings contradict each other, and nobody

236
00:17:24,383 --> 00:17:27,152
sees it, because each looks correct alone.

237
00:17:27,492 --> 00:17:32,244
The fix keeps the saving. The workflow
now starts on every pull request.

238
00:17:32,284 --> 00:17:37,635
A first job computes whether code changed.
The build and test jobs run only if it

239
00:17:37,675 --> 00:17:43,159
did. They are skipped by a condition
otherwise. A final job depends on all of them,

240
00:17:43,199 --> 00:17:47,885
runs always, and fails if any job
that ran has failed or was cancelled.

241
00:17:48,225 --> 00:17:53,354
The ruleset requires that one final job, by
its name, and the team pins the check to

242
00:17:53,394 --> 00:17:58,401
the expected source app, because an unpinned
check can be set by anyone with write

243
00:17:58,441 --> 00:17:59,077
permission.

244
00:17:59,417 --> 00:18:04,117
They state in the design note what the
textbook states: this pattern is an inference

245
00:18:04,157 --> 00:18:08,687
from the documented behaviour of skipped
workflows and skipped jobs, not a GitHub

246
00:18:08,727 --> 00:18:10,718
recipe. The Actions videos build it.

247
00:18:12,558 --> 00:18:18,320
Your turn. Do Exercise 23.3, "Review this
ruleset", in exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

248
00:18:18,360 --> 00:18:24,053
For each rule and sub-option in the ruleset
you are given, write down the loophole

249
00:18:24,093 --> 00:18:29,296
it closes and the cost it adds, and
mark every place where two settings can

250
00:18:29,336 --> 00:18:34,120
contradict each other, as the path
filter and the required check did.

251
00:18:34,460 --> 00:18:39,484
The challenge is Exercise 23.6, "Four pull
requests that will not merge", in the

252
00:18:39,524 --> 00:18:44,674
same file. Diagnose each from the rules to
the cause, with the pre-flight commands

253
00:18:44,714 --> 00:18:45,751
where they apply.

254
00:18:47,591 --> 00:18:50,801
Question 249 of the CTO question bank:

255
00:18:53,141 --> 00:18:57,864
Answer out loud. A strong answer states
what the rule actually tests, in terms of

256
00:18:57,904 --> 00:19:01,981
names and commits, and why "never
started" is different from "failed".

257
00:19:02,021 --> 00:19:06,450
It distinguishes a skipped workflow from
a skipped job, with the status each

258
00:19:06,490 --> 00:19:11,390
reports. The design it proposes moves the
decision inside the workflow, requires one

259
00:19:11,430 --> 00:19:15,977
stable name, and says what protects that
name. It is clear about which part is

260
00:19:16,017 --> 00:19:18,506
documented and which
is a design inference.

261
00:19:20,346 --> 00:19:25,211
Let's land this. A blocked merge is a list
of lookups, and you can now run half of

262
00:19:25,251 --> 00:19:27,305
them before you open the merge box.

263
00:19:27,645 --> 00:19:29,305
You should now be able to say:

264
00:19:29,645 --> 00:19:34,791
The pull request rule has sub-options that
each close one loophole: approvals, stale

265
00:19:34,831 --> 00:19:39,853
dismissal, last-push approval, code owners,
dismissal restrictions, conversations,

266
00:19:39,893 --> 00:19:41,890
merge methods, reviewers by path.

267
00:19:42,230 --> 00:19:47,000
Required status checks are matched by name
on the newest commit; a skipped workflow

268
00:19:47,040 --> 00:19:49,781
stays pending and a skipped
job reports success.

269
00:19:50,121 --> 00:19:53,500
"Up to date" means the tip of the
base is an ancestor of the head.

270
00:19:53,840 --> 00:19:58,489
Signed commits are checked on the commits
a pull request introduces; rebase and

271
00:19:58,529 --> 00:20:03,355
merge cannot satisfy the rule; linear
history forbids merge commits on the target.

272
00:20:03,695 --> 00:20:08,339
Ancestry, conflicts, merge commits,
signatures and paths can be checked locally

273
00:20:08,379 --> 00:20:10,058
before you ask for the merge.

274
00:20:11,898 --> 00:20:16,335
Read sections 18.6 to 18.11 of Chapter 18.

275
00:20:16,675 --> 00:20:20,940
That was the densest list in this
module, and you got through it.

276
00:20:20,980 --> 00:20:26,370
Practise with Exercise 23.3. Next: tag
rulesets, push rulesets, and classic branch

277
00:20:26,410 --> 00:20:30,608
protection. Until then, look at
the state first and type second.

278
00:20:30,648 --> 00:20:32,198
See you in the next one.
